#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115491 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186111955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115491 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115491 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115491 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d5/d53e300cf45128d4d0d3e06e9c4adc6ab966ff9493c399496d18a2e2bf634099.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={105}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "784a5981-ef45-51c5-80cb-3129df419fa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115491 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{105}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={75}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{75}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{165}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={15}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116268 #edb/ref 1061 36502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116268 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cedeba2-5a6c-5062-880b-beb7fa0aa70f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116268 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1061 9151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4231f520-72fe-5c14-8733-25cc76a6e65e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1061 9154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52e4c58c-e58e-5223-98e5-cc31383de47c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1061 9158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9f034d0-1d0e-54ea-83b8-ad683048d5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1061 9160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25c91feb-798e-5857-9090-3b36088bc71b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ shown below. \n\n![](images/6f/6fdb9a8337d16f1ef1910989b3df92844c5393c33108f98ae7993cc939bed0b3.png)\n\nNow, let's draw a line segment that \n\n- runs from $\\color{blue}C$ to the line ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}},$ and\n\n- is *perpendicular* to ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\nThis line segment is the dashed segment shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4a7ee2771c9b0c60dbb07c43b0439e822ea9b2c261ac41af9b44fec72b9f2a91.png)\n\nFinally, let's label the other endpoint of our segment ${\\color{blue}{H}}.$\n\n![](images/75/758eab533719220515460b6677054438a9ac0e2701d1a5267b973e526f63bd90.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The line segment ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}$ is called the **height** (or **altitude**) *corresponding to* ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\n- The line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is called the **base** of the altitude ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}.$\n\nAs we'll soon discover, the heights and their corresponding bases are important attributes of triangles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1065 2232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bdb9bfbd-b830-51f0-8712-5d34f83f2812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1064 "We can draw exactly three heights (or altitudes) in any triangle. However, sometimes the height lies outside the triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/df/df1ac2c1074305f8ead506d9aeb8c3927ad51ae5949b45976f1971b52d402ee5.png)\n\nThe height corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nIn this case, it's impossible to draw a perpendicular from $A$ to the *segment* $\\overline{BC}.$ However, we can first *extend* the line segment $\\overline{BC}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26bf9eb8af1e9b71c263c08cae5797d5c911410985cdc01213ac2c295f7b8d46.png)\n\nNow, we draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$ This gives the height $\\overline{AH}{:}$\n\n![](images/89/894686fce1393ebcf998be6885020627c9f3cde5916adfec67234f15f8895826.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1065 2236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4031d801-f4b8-543f-ad4d-9ef60d513427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1067 "Case of Extended Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386882 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/55/5548481ccbe3aee88fd4b346ad53777b7101223c19f7e8408c8ef85bb0d9df25.png)\n\nGiven the right triangle $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f1782d4-ceb0-5897-9f90-478b4c1242ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386882 #edb/ref 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301c31fa98ce5c016337f422566b94a742e3da126b76f9060e4b13d6a8e86358.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186386888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386883 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "05bed876-047b-5aad-854a-94fee100e605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386884 #edb/ref 1080 "images/16/16c9990277df1c1b212ff154c4951b59301ef5b7f8360f82b041e73a5a3a984b.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386884 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386884 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b161b259-2a2c-531b-b051-34dd949dc4d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386885 #edb/ref 1080 "images/10/10e9f5a6af4d6d2b9c6392de91432b09620107d3dc645fc6ca65fdbda52f7c83.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386885 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386885 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e0792fd2-fe4a-5351-bd8d-d02531595632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386886 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ce/ce3493e78660cfa84515970053105b95df5542eafbc4630c1e5b0afe9f658036.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386886 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386886 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b85c7ea1-4023-5d0d-86b4-0459134b26d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386887 #ed